Found while closing karr #34.

File::SOPS::edit wraps the reparse of the edited text in an eval and reports any failure as "The edited document does not parse (REASON)". Since #34 made a non-mapping top-level sops entry a parse-time refusal, an edited document containing e.g. "sops: mine" now takes that branch - so the user is told the document does not parse when it parses perfectly well, and the dedicated message immediately below ("The edited document has a top-level sops entry. That name is reserved for the metadata section, which is written back automatically -- remove it and edit again.") is unreachable for every shape of that entry except a mapping.

The REASON text is interpolated, so the message is still actionable and the file is left unchanged either way. Only the framing is wrong.

Fix candidate: distinguish the two inside the eval, or let the reserved-key refusal out of the eval and keep it for the dedicated croak. Both touch edit only.
Direct consequence of the karr #34 fix committed as edb7444 -- our own regression, introduced hours ago, so it should not outlive the release that introduces it. Sequenced for tonight.
Fixed in the working tree, NOT committed. edit now classifies the exception that comes out of the format handler's parse: File::SOPS::_is_sops_not_a_mapping matches the refusal Metadata::from_hash raises, so every shape of a top-level 'sops' entry in the edited text (scalar, list, null, mapping) reaches the dedicated reserved-key message, and only text that really does not parse gets the parse message. The message itself moved into _edited_sops_key_reserved, shared by both call sites. Chose classification over 'distinguish inside the eval' because the format handlers offer no syntax-only parse and adding one is the format lane's -- recorded as karr #51 with the structural shape (a read-side counterpart to emit, so edit could load-then-validate the way sops does).

Measured on sops 3.13.3 in editor mode, which draws the same line and is shape-independent too: 'Tree not valid for encryption' plus the reserved-key text for sops: mine, a list, a null and a mapping alike, against 'Could not load tree, probably due to invalid syntax' for broken YAML. Recorded in the code comment, the POD and the Changes entry.

Regression test in both directions in t/17-in-place-and-edit.t: the sops-entry subtest now loops over all four shapes and asserts the dedicated message AND that it is not framed as a parse failure; the broken-YAML subtest gained an unlike() so the fix cannot over-correct. Against HEAD 3e4f3bb: 6 assertions fail (scalar, list and null x 2 each), one failing subtest, 1 of 26 top-level tests -- the mapping shape passes there, which is exactly the shape-dependence the ticket describes.

Full suite with the binary, SOPS_BIN=$HOME/bin/sops prove -lr t/: 21 files, 499 tests, all pass. t/04-interop.t RAN (reported 'Using sops binary: /storage/raid/home/getty/bin/sops', sops 3.13.3) and so did the two sops -d subtests at the end of t/17. t/14-encryption-rules.t was green this run; the karr #48 flake did not fire and nothing there was touched.

